.TH SYS 1 2026-09-15 "sys" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sys \- set up, sync and check this Mac
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sys
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sys
is the single entry point to the configuration repository in
.IR ~/.sys .
It sets up a new machine, keeps an existing one in step with the repository,
saves local changes back, and reports on what is installed.
.PP
The repository is the source of truth. Anything it tracks \(em dotfiles, colour
palettes, editor settings, the Dock, SSH access \(em is mirrored onto the machine by
.BR "sys sync" ,
and mirrored back by
.BR "sys push" .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B setup
Set up this machine from scratch. Installs Homebrew and everything in the
.IR Brewfile ,
copies dotfiles into place, installs language versions with
.BR mise (1),
downloads and verifies MailExporter from its GitHub release, builds Paywall Down
from its private repo (macOS scheme only) and installs it in
.IR /Applications ,
installs App Store apps and
applies macOS defaults. Finishes by printing the steps that need a human.
.IP
Setup also turns on Remote Login (SSH) and Screen Sharing (VNC) for you, using launchd directly, since
.B systemsetup
needs Full Disk Access on current macOS.
.IP
Your password is asked for once, at the start, and used for the whole run:
it is handed to sudo through a helper that is deleted when setup finishes, so
Homebrew and the casks never prompt again.
.IP
Safe to re-run. Use
.B \-\-mas
to run only that part.
.TP
.BR sync " [" all ]
Pull the latest configuration and apply it here. With
.BR all ,
then do the same on every machine this one may log into according to
.IR config/ssh/access ,
one after another over ssh; a machine that is off or unreachable is skipped
with a note.
.IP
If the pull updates
.B sys
itself, it restarts with the new version, since bash has already parsed the
running copy and the change would otherwise only take effect next time.
.IP
It reconciles with the remote in whichever direction is needed: pulls when
behind, pushes when ahead, and rebases your commits on top when both have
moved. It only pushes commits that already exist — recording this machine's
state into new commits is
.BR "sys push" ,
so sync never commits your files on your behalf. The one exception is this
machine's own SSH public key, which it shares into
.I config/ssh/keys/
by itself; see
.BR ssh .
.IP
If the
.I Brewfile
differs from the one last installed here, the added and removed lines are
shown and the missing packages installed — as you, with no stored password;
an app that truly needs an admin password asks for it itself. Nothing is ever
uninstalled: removing a line stops a new machine getting it, but leaves
existing machines alone. The Dock and desktop colour are rebuilt the same
way, whenever
.I config/dock
or
.I config/desktop-colour
changes. Paywall Down is updated the same way: the private repo is
fast-forwarded and the macOS app rebuilt when that commit changed.
.TP
.B push
Save this machine's configuration into the repository, commit and push.
.TP
.B extras
Install the optional extras listed in
.I Brewfile.optional
and
.IR config/mas\-apps\-optional.txt .
Never run by
.BR "sys setup" ;
these are large or occasional installs you ask for explicitly.
.TP
.B remove
Undo everything setup installed: Homebrew and its packages, mise toolchains,
the dotfiles it copied, editor configuration and the Dock. Lists what it will remove and asks before doing
anything;
.B \-\-dryrun
shows the list and stops.
.IP
It does not erase macOS and does not touch your user account, SSH or Screen
Sharing, so a headless machine stays reachable and you can install again
straight away.
.TP
.BR hostname " [" \fIname\fR ]
Set the computer name. With a name, sets it directly; without one, shows the
current name and asks, falling back after fifteen seconds to
.IR dwkns\-type\-chip ,
such as dwkns\-mbp\-m1 or dwkns\-mini\-m4. Setup asks the same question once.
.IP
All three of
.IR ComputerName ,
.I HostName
and
.I LocalHostName
are set to the same value, lowercased, with spaces turned to hyphens and other
punctuation dropped, since scutil rejects those. "Dazza's Mac Mini" becomes
dazzas\-mac\-mini everywhere.
.TP
.B ssh
List the machines this one can reach over SSH: the aliases sys wrote from
.IR config/ssh/access ,
then anything hand-written in
.I ~/.ssh/config
(flagged when its address is not on your tailnet). Read live from the machine.
.PP
Every
.B sys sync
also applies
.IR config/ssh/access ,
which says who may log in where and as which account. On each machine that
means: make its ed25519 key in
.I ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
if it has none; share its public keys as
.I config/ssh/keys/NAME.pub
(the user key) and
.I config/ssh/hostkeys/NAME.pub
(the sshd host key), committing and pushing those files \(em the only thing
sync ever commits by itself; install the keys of the machines the access file
lets in here, in a marked block of
.I ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
that leaves anything outside the block alone; pin every other machine's host
key in
.IR ~/.ssh/known_hosts_sys ,
so a wrong machine is refused and a first contact needs no question; and write
.I ~/.ssh/config.d/sys
with an alias and account for every other machine, so
.B ssh mini
reaches
.B dwkns\-mini
as the right user. Each alias tries the Tailscale name first and, if that does
not answer on port 22 within two seconds, falls back to
.IR NAME.local ,
so it works on the home network with Tailscale off and from anywhere with it
on. The aliases never offer a password: a machine that refuses the key is
either not allowed by the access file or not the machine it claims to be.
Plain
.B ssh user@host
still can. A machine whose host key is not in the repo yet is accepted on
first contact until it is. A single
.B Include config.d/*
line is added at the top of
.IR ~/.ssh/config ;
the rest of that file is yours.
.PP
The key has no passphrase, deliberately: the disk is encrypted, a headless
machine has nobody to type one, and the point is that ssh just works. The
private key never leaves the machine that made it. Nothing here ever types a
password. Sharing the keys needs a GitHub login on this machine; a machine
without one is signed in on the spot, once, with a code and a browser page.
.TP
.B ssh NAME
Log in to NAME (the alias or the full machine name from
.IR config/ssh/access )
and attach a tmux session that no other connection is using, or start one.
A plain interactive
.B ssh
login does the same, through a hook at the end of
.I ~/.zshrc
on a Mac and a marked block sync keeps in
.I ~/.bashrc
on Linux. The rule is
.IR ~/.config/tmux/attach .
.B Ctrl\-b d
detaches and leaves the session running.
.TP
.B ssh harden
Optional. Turn off password logins on this machine by writing
.I /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/00\-sys\-keys\-only.conf
and restarting sshd. Refuses to run until at least one machine is let in
here, so a headless machine cannot lock itself out. Open sessions are not
dropped. Passwords stay on unless you run this.
.TP
.B tailscale up
Turn Tailscale on here, then on every machine this one may log into according
to
.IR config/ssh/access .
This machine goes first, because away from home the tailnet is the only route
to the others; at home the aliases fall back to
.IR NAME.local ,
so a machine with Tailscale off can still be reached. A Mac opens the
Tailscale app and runs
.BR "tailscale up" ;
Linux starts
.B tailscaled
and runs
.B tailscale up
through passwordless sudo. With no flags,
.B tailscale up
keeps every setting and only reconnects. A machine listed in
.I config/ssh/no\-sys
(the NAS) is only checked: its Tailscale needs sudo with a password, so the
command to type is printed instead.
.TP
.B doctor
Check that the expected tools are installed, report language versions, whether
the App Store is reachable, whether this machine's SSH keys are shared and
who is let in, whether sshd still allows passwords, whether the Dock and
desktop colour match their config, whether Paywall Down is installed, and
whether the repository has
uncommitted changes. Changes nothing.
.TP
.BR net " [" \fIname\fR ]
List the network shares in
.I config/net
and say which are mounted. With a name, connect to that one:
.B sys net dwkns\-nas
hands the url to Finder, which authenticates from the login Keychain, and waits
for the volume to appear. Passwords are never stored or typed by
.BR sys .
.TP
.BR ssh " " all
The same list, plus any host written by hand in
.IR ~/.ssh/config .
Each machine's two routes are probed: its Tailscale name and
.IR NAME.local
on the home network. Tailscale being off is reported once, at the top.
.TP
.B ai
Print a brief of the whole repository for an AI: every command, what each
file does, the config files and their formats, what sync copies where, the
machines, the local state, and the conventions to follow when changing it.
Generated from the repository itself, so it cannot drift from the code. Plain
text, no colour.
.TP
.BR nas " [" open ]
Show the NAS: whether it can be reached over the local network and the
tailnet, its model and DSM version, how full its disk is, whether Tailscale is
running on it, and its shares \(em with any USB drive shown against the share
that serves it. Read live over one SSH call, without root.
.B sys nas open
opens its web page in the browser. Both read
.IR config/nas .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Show what would happen without changing anything. Accepted by every command,
and leaves no "already done" marks behind.
.TP
.BR \-\-yes ", " \-y
Do not ask for confirmation
.RB ( remove ).
.TP
.B \-\-force
.RB ( sync )
Reinstall every tracked file, run the Brewfile, and rebuild the Dock and
desktop colour even when nothing has changed.
.SH RELOADING YOUR SHELL
.B sys
runs as its own process, so it cannot reload the shell you started it from.
The
.B sys
function in
.I ~/.aliases
wraps it: when setup or sync changes anything, it reloads your shell with
.B exec zsh
once
.B sys
has finished successfully. Run from the one-line installer, setup starts a
fresh login shell itself.
.SH HOW SYNCING WORKS
.B sys sync
mirrors three directories:
.PP
.RS
.nf
dotfiles              \(-> ~
colors                \(-> ~/Library/Colors
config/sublime\-config \(-> ~/Library/Application Support/Sublime Text/User
config/cursor         \(-> ~/Library/Application Support/Cursor/User
.fi
.RE
.PP
The contents of each repository directory are the manifest. To track a new file,
put it in the right directory; there is no list to update. To stop tracking one,
delete it.
.PP
The repository always wins. Before replacing a system file whose contents
differ, the old version is copied to
.I ~/.sys/.drift
and a note is printed, so a local edit you forgot to
.B sys push
is never lost.
.I .drift
is overwritten each run rather than accumulating; git is the history.
.SH EDITORS
VS Code has Settings Sync, so its settings, keybindings and extensions follow
your account and are deliberately not tracked here.
.PP
Cursor does not ship Settings Sync, so its
.I settings.json
and
.I keybindings.json
are mirrored from
.IR config/cursor ,
and its extensions are listed in
.IR config/cursor\-extensions.txt .
.B sys setup
installs any that are missing;
.B sys push
refreshes the list so it cannot drift.
.SH LANGUAGES
.BR mise (1)
owns Ruby, Node and Python. Global versions live in
.IR ~/.config/mise/config.toml ;
override them per project with a local
.IR mise.toml .
After changing a version, run
.B sys push
then
.BR "mise install" .
Python packages and virtual environments go through
.BR uv (1).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.sys/Brewfile
Applications and command-line tools.
.TP
.I ~/.sys/macos.sh
macOS defaults.
.TP
.I ~/.sys/config/mas\-apps.txt
App Store application IDs.
.TP
.I ~/.sys/config/input
Keyboard repeat, trackpad speed, clicking and gestures, applied immediately
rather than at next login. Applied by
.B sys setup
and on every
.BR "sys sync" .
.TP
.I ~/.sys/config/desktop-colour
Solid desktop colour as four numbers, set through macOS's wallpaper API and
checked on screen afterwards. Applied once by
.BR "sys setup" .
.TP
.I ~/.sys/Brewfile.optional
Optional extras, installed only by
.BR "sys extras" .
.TP
.I ~/.sys/config/mas\-apps\-optional.txt
Optional App Store apps, installed only by
.BR "sys extras" .
.TP
.I ~/.sys/dotfiles/
Copied into the home directory.
.TP
.I ~/.config/tmux/attach
Picks the tmux session an ssh login attaches to: one nobody else is using, or
a new one. Also copied to Linux machines.
.TP
.I ~/.sys/.drift/
System files replaced by the last sync. Not tracked by git.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ROOT_DIR
Location of the repository. Default
.IR ~/.sys .
.TP
.B DRY_RUN
Set to 1 for the effect of
.BR \-\-dry\-run .
.TP
.B ASSUME_YES
Set to 1 for the effect of
.BR \-\-yes .
.SH EXAMPLES
Set up a brand new Mac:
.PP
.RS
.nf
curl \-fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dwkns/\\
system\-install/master/install.sh | bash
.fi
.RE
.PP
See what a sync would change, without changing it:
.PP
.RS
.B sys sync \-\-dry\-run
.RE
.PP
Save a change made directly to a system file:
.PP
.RS
.B sys push
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mise (1),
.BR uv (1),
.BR brew (1),
.BR mas (1),
.BR defaults (1),
.BR security (1)
.PP
.I ~/.sys/README.md
